WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.617
A correlated query becomes easier if you trace one outer row at a time.

00:00:04.617 --> 00:00:07.632
Imagine the current row is a Glucose test.

00:00:07.632 --> 00:00:12.717
The inner query uses the outer test name to average only Glucose values.

00:00:12.717 --> 00:00:16.322
Then the current result is compared with that benchmark.

00:00:16.322 --> 00:00:21.532
When the outer row changes to another test, the inner benchmark changes as well.

00:00:21.532 --> 00:00:26.007
A correlated subquery refers to the current row of the outer query.

00:00:26.007 --> 00:00:31.022
Conceptually, the inner query is evaluated in relation to each outer row.

00:00:31.022 --> 00:00:36.217
That makes some business rules easy to express, but it can be expensive at scale.

00:00:36.217 --> 00:00:41.241
Compare a correlated formulation with a pre-aggregated join when performance matters.
